sweet-wlan-irq-ack-once (lowclk 2026-11-14, fix plan 0349 after boot B10b; revised after review 0349): one 0xC30 ack per interrupt entry, Linux-style, and a stuck rule that cannot fire under steady traffic. Changes bsd/dev/sweet_wlan_irq.h (the pure core created by patch 0347) and, for one GAP text and two comments, bsd/dev/sweet_wlan.c.
Observation [obs: hardware, boot B10b, job c17e, run resident-213410-b2, 2026-11-13]: with sweetwifiirq=1 the doorbell armed and took 9138 interrupts in 157 s (0 spurious, 0 storm, 4 missed); then swi_ack_loop printed `SWEET WLAN IRQ GAP: 0xC30 not zero after ack and drain (a=0x1 b=0)`, the mode turned itself off as off:c30stuck and the 5 ms poll carried the data path.
Cause [read: 0347 swi_ack_loop]: the loop re-read 0xC30 after each ack + drain and called it stuck after SWI_ACK_TRIES (8) rounds with a bit still set. Bit 0 of 0xC30 is BRCMF_PCIE_64_MB_INT_D2H0_DB0, an ordinary D2H ring doorbell that the dongle re-raises while traffic flows, so 8 non-zero reads in a row happen under steady load without any fault [read: Linux v6.6 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/pcie.c, rev-64 mailbox block].
Linux brcmf_pcie_isr_thread (pcie.c:939-963 v6.6) reads MAILBOXINT once, writes the same bits back (W1C), handles them and re-enables the interrupt; it does not loop until zero and has no stuck verdict, a doorbell still pending re-fires the level line [read: pcie.c v6.6].
What changed: (1) doorbell mode, per interrupt entry: swi_pre reads 0xC30 once and W1Cs exactly the bits read (as in 0347), the glue drains the rings once, swi_post no longer runs the ack loop (swi_ack_loop is gone from the thread path); ORDER stays ack, drain, unmask. A failed or refused 0xC30 read or W1C of an entry is reported by swi_post (GAP read/write/refused text of 0347) and turns the mode off as c30. An all-ones 0xC30 read (0xFFFFFFFF, dead link) is not acked and turns the mode off at the first entry (review F3).
(2) new stuck rule: c30stuck only when 0xC30 is non-zero at entry on SWI_C30_RUN_MAX (64) consecutive interrupt entries AND every one of those entries' drains found no ring work; an entry whose drain found work, ANY pass that drained work (review F2: the poll drains first, the interrupt pass then finds the doorbell bit with nothing left), or a zero read resets the counter. GAP text key unchanged ("0xC30 not zero after ack and drain"), args a = last 0xC30 value, b = the consecutive count.
(3) review F1: before the unmask swi_post reads 0xC30 once more (the read row 0347 already has). Non-zero = a bit set between the entry's read and its W1C is still asserted, which an edge-latched ELBI bit 14 would never signal again: no unmask, pend is set, the next pass is a new entry (ack, drain, this check). Bounded by the c30 run (no-work entries) and the thread pass rate; the line stays masked meanwhile, the poll carries the data. A failed read or all-ones here turns the mode off as c30. Counter rechecks counts the postponed unmasks.
(4) review F1: "doorbell silent" auto-off: SWI_SILENT_MAX (200) poll passes that found ring work with no interrupt pass while irqs did not rise since the last pass (an interrupt resets the run), then GAP "doorbell silent: poll finds work, no interrupt" (a = passes, b = irqs) and mode off:silent. Row added to the GAP table of sw_irq_note in sweet_wlan.c. The noirq rule (irqs == 0 ever) is unchanged and fires first at the same count.
(5) swi_ack_loop stays for ARM only (step 2, while 0xC34 = 0 and the SPI is disabled, so it cannot spin an interrupt), unchanged: 8 rounds. The stale "re-read loop" comments in sweet_wlan_irq.h and the three in sweet_wlan.c are reworded.
Known and left (review F5, pre-existing in 0347): entries with no work that alternate 0xC30 non-zero / zero trip neither the empty rule nor the c30 rule; only the storm guard (5000 ISR entries/s) bounds them.
What did not change: storm 5000/s, noirq after 200 poll-found-work passes, spurious 3, empty-interrupt run 64, elbistuck, the gate and its closes, teardown order, the judge rows and value rules, the arm order, the keep-thread glue and the log lines other than the new GAP row. New struct swi fields: c30_run, p_aerr, silent_run, silent_seen, rechecks.
No new register access: reads and writes stay exactly 0xC30 read + W1C of the bits read (judge rows of 0347), the ring drain and the ELBI/0xC34 accesses of 0347; no new offset, no new access kind, no new value. The one added 0xC30 read (before the unmask) uses the existing read row; the doorbell-mode thread path still has fewer 0xC30 accesses than 0347's loop (one read + one W1C + one read per entry instead of a loop of them).
Host test: tools/test_wlan_irq_core.py applies this patch on top of the 0347 core (same chaining as shim/build.sh for 0124 + 0174) and checks: a steady stream of non-zero 0xC30 with work never disables; 63 non-zero + no-work entries then work resets; 64 disables as c30stuck with the GAP; one entry = one 0xC30 read + one W1C (+ the pre-unmask read); the poll-drains-first sequence (review R3) never disables; a bit set between the read and the W1C postpones the unmask and the silent doorbell ends in the GAP and off:silent (R4); all-ones read; a failing W1C driven through the ops fake; mutants of the new rules are killed.
Review: the 0347 write path was reviewed (docs/reviews/0346-wifi-irq-review.md); this patch adds no write and removes accesses; its own review is docs/reviews/0349-wifi-irq-ack-once-review.md (findings F1-F6 fixed here, F5 left). NOT RUN, NOT BUILT, NOT ON HARDWARE: the glue was not compiled with 0349. ORDER: after 0347.
